        <div id="grips-vs-strap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Hand Grips vs Chest Strap: Why the Strap Matters</h2>
<p>
            Contact grips measure the tiny electrical signal of your heartbeat through your palms. They need both hands
            held still on the metal, which is fine at a walk and nearly impossible at a run, when your arms swing and
            your hands sweat. A chest strap sits against your skin over the heart and reads the same signal
            continuously, without you touching anything.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Method</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Works at a run?</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Good for</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hand grips</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Poorly; you must hold on</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">An occasional check at a walk</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Chest strap (Bluetooth)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heart-rate programs and zone training</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wrist watch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually, with more lag</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Logging; less reliable for intervals</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Arm band</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">People who dislike chest straps</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">chest strap vs wrist heart rate</a> guide explains the accuracy gap in detail. If the grips on your current treadmill read nonsense, our <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate not working</a> guide covers the common fixes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hr-programs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Heart-Rate Programs vs ActivePulse: What Each Does</h2>
<p>
            There are two ways a treadmill can use your heart rate, and they differ in cost and control.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Built-in heart-rate programs</strong> (Horizon, Sole, <a href="/lifesmart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>LifeSmart</a>). You set a target heart rate or
            zone, and the program adjusts the workout to keep you there. No subscription is needed, and the program
            runs on the console. Horizon lists Heart Rate and Custom HR on the 7.4 AT and Target HRT on the 7.0 AT; Sole
            lists two heart-rate programs on the F63; LifeSmart lists three heart-rate-controlled programs on the
            AT1 Pro.</li>
            <li><strong>ActivePulse</strong> (NordicTrack, ProForm). iFIT&rsquo;s feature changes speed and incline
            automatically to keep you in a heart-rate zone during iFIT workouts, using a compatible Bluetooth strap
            sold separately. The listings say it needs an iFIT membership: Pro on the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial 1750</a>, Train on the
            Carbon TL.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Which suits you depends on whether you want classes. If you only want to train by zone, a built-in
            program is the cheaper long-term choice; see <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>.
            If you want trainer-led classes that also steer by heart rate, ActivePulse is the more automatic option; <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is iFIT worth it</a> weighs the membership.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="zones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Using Heart Rate on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            A heart-rate monitor is only useful if the targets are right. The common formula, 220 minus your age, is
            a rough population estimate and can be well off for an individual, and medicines such as beta blockers
            change heart rate entirely. Our <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-zones/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate zones</a> guide explains better ways to set zones, and <a href="/zone-2-training-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">zone 2 training on a treadmill</a> covers the most popular use of a strap.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pair before you start.</strong> Moisten the strap&rsquo;s sensor pads, put it on, then start the
            treadmill so the console can find it.</li>
            <li><strong>One device at a time.</strong> Many straps connect to only one or two devices over Bluetooth. If
            your watch or phone grabs it first, the treadmill may not see it.</li>
            <li><strong>Give programs time.</strong> Heart rate lags effort by a minute or so, so heart-rate programs
            change speed or incline gradually. That is normal, not a fault.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            This page is about equipment. If you have a heart condition or take medicines that affect heart rate, ask
            your doctor what range is safe for you before training to a number.
          </p>
        </div>

