        <div id="pairing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Will My Own Strap or Watch Work?</h2>
<p>
            Probably, if it broadcasts heart rate over Bluetooth, but check before you rely on it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bluetooth chest straps</strong> from the main brands broadcast a standard heart-rate signal that
            most current consoles can read. Some older treadmills use a different wireless receiver, so check the manual
            for the type the console supports.</li>
            <li><strong>Watches</strong> do not always broadcast heart rate to other devices by default. On many you must
            turn on a heart-rate broadcast setting, and some cannot do it at all.</li>
            <li><strong>Apple Watch</strong> pairs with GymKit-equipped machines such as the Horizon 7.0 AT, per Horizon.</li>
            <li><strong>ActivePulse</strong> needs a compatible Bluetooth monitor; iFIT sells one, and the listings say the
            monitor is sold separately.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If your treadmill already reads heart rate badly, see <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate not working</a> before replacing anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grip-tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Getting a Better Reading From Hand Grips</h2>
<p>
            If you already own a treadmill with only grip sensors, you can get a more usable number from them before
            buying a strap. Slow to a walk, hold both sensors with your whole palm, keep your hands still and wait 10 to 15
            seconds for the reading to settle. Dry, cold or very sweaty hands all make readings worse, so a quick wipe helps.
            Treat the number as a spot check, not a live training signal.
          </p>
          <p>
            For anything that needs heart rate continuously, such as zone 2 sessions, intervals or a heart-rate program
            that adjusts the treadmill for you, a chest strap is the practical answer. It is also the cheapest upgrade on
            this page: it turns most of these treadmills from a machine that can show heart rate into one that can train
            by it.
          </p>
        </div>

