          <h2>Buying a Treadmill for Heart-Rate Training? Avoid These 4 Mistakes</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from trusting the grips or missing a membership requirement.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Relying on the hand grips</h3>
              <p>Grip sensors need still hands on the metal and fail at a run. If heart rate matters to you, budget for a chest strap or buy a treadmill that includes one.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Missing the membership requirement</h3>
              <p>ActivePulse on NordicTrack and ProForm needs an iFIT membership. Check the listing’s small print, and compare the yearly cost against a subscription-free machine with built-in heart-rate programs.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Using 220 minus age as gospel</h3>
              <p>That formula is a rough estimate and can be well off for you, and some medicines change heart rate. Set zones from a better method, and ask your doctor if you have a heart condition.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Letting the watch grab the strap</h3>
              <p>Many straps connect to one or two devices. If your watch or phone pairs first, the treadmill may not see the strap. Pair the treadmill first, or check the strap allows several connections.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
